 ],
 "annotations": [
  {
   "id": "n-land",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-land",
   "text": "First, because it is the first thing anyone meets \u2014 and <b>every row below is one screen at both widths</b>: 1440 light, 360 light, 1440 dark, 360 dark. Built from real components at their real size, fragments rather than whole screens, and on the 1440 board from the WEB app's own components, because a laptop reader must see the tables and the dashboard they will actually get. <b>First launch</b> is the screen an <i>installed</i> app shows: v1 is a PWA (spec \u00a7G, <i>the app is installable</i>), and an installed app opens cold \u2014 no address bar, no landing page, nothing to have scrolled. It is not a mobile screen; it is what any installed copy shows on a device with no budget on it yet. `LandMotion` draws the pointer states, because a static artboard cannot show motion and the build cannot implement from a description. Two image slots are specified and not filled \u2014 `docs/design/image-brief.md` carries the shot list, the licences and the reject list."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-sec",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-sec",
   "text": "<b>Off page 1, 27 September.</b> It has a route, it is reached from three places \u2014 the landing page, the sign-up tick-box and Settings \u2014 and it was living inside the landing page only because that is where it was first drawn. <b>\u00a710.1\u2019s sentence is here once</b> and nowhere else composes its own version of it: <i>We never store your bank data in readable form.</i> The row below it is the same page as a modal, which is what sign-up opens rather than repeating the wording inline \u2014 five copies of a promise is five chances to get it wrong. <b>The build guard fails on the sentence this replaced</b>, which was false the day ADR-009 gave v1 a server."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-w-in",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-w-in",
   "text": "The whole account flow, then the six setting-up steps, <b>every screen on its own row at both widths</b> \u2014 because a six-step flow drawn as one picture is a picture, not a flow, and two widths on separate pages is how they drift. They did: the 1440 steps were wrong on four of six, and the 360 screen had each of those four right the whole time. <b>Sign-up no longer carries a trust panel.</b> It carries the one fixed claim (tokens \u00a710.1) and a link that opens security and privacy as a modal \u2014 one copy of the wording instead of five \u2014 plus an explicit tick-box, because consent you have to tick is consent and a sentence above a button is not. <i>Forgot password</i> and <i>Reset password</i> are three screens, not one \u2014 ask, wait, set \u2014 because the step in the middle is where resets actually fail. The three 360-only rows are sheets a phone needs and a laptop does not."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-set",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-set",
   "text": "Data, cycle and categories, and <b>the signed-out row is the important one</b>: the free tier is not a trial, so the screen a person sees without an account is a working screen, not a wall. <b>Settings is the sidebar\u2019s bottom group at 1440</b> (\u00a72, amended 26 September) \u2014 the six above it are places the money is; this is where you change how the app behaves, and a flat list of seven gave a utility equal billing with the thing the product is for. 360 had it in the More sheet all along."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-tier",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-tier",
   "text": "What paying buys, in the two states a person meets it in. <b>There is no lock anywhere on this page</b>: no padlock, no grey-out, no crown, and the word <i>unlock</i> appears nowhere \u2014 item 24, and the build guard checks the vocabulary. Paying buys <b>reach</b> \u2014 a second device, a second person, bank movement \u2014 and never opinions about someone\u2019s money. <b>Lapsed keeps everything readable</b>: the data is theirs, and a product that holds a budget hostage has stopped being a budgeting app."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-hh",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-hh",
   "text": "Two people, one expense, two different figures \u2014 and the screen\u2019s job is to let them agree, not to referee. <b>Both figures are drawn as equals</b>: same size, same weight, no rose on either, no \u201ccorrect\u201d one. <b><i>Leave it for now</i> carries the same weight as the rest</b>, because the honest outcome is often that it waits for a conversation the app is not in."
  },
  {
   "id": "n-imp",
   "x": 0,
   "y": -250,
   "w": 820,
   "page": "page-imp",
